==Icelandic==

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|kenna|t1=to teach|ari|t2=-er|lang=is}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈcʰɛnːaːrɪ/|lang=is}}
*: {{rhymes|ɛnːaːrɪ|lang=is}}

===Noun===
{{is-noun|m}}

# [[teacher]]
#: ''Hún er [[góður]] '''kennari'''.''
#:: She is a [[good]] '''teacher'''.
#: ''[[Jónas]] er [[undarlegur]] '''kennari''', en samt [[skemmtilegur]].'
#:: Mister [[Jónas]] is an [[odd]] '''teacher''', but [[fun]] nevertheless.

====Declension====
{{is-decl-noun-m-w1|k|e|nn|a|r}}

====Usage notes====
Like most occupational terms, ''kennari'', although grammatically masculine, may be used for either sex. The term {{term|kennslukona|lang=is}} (literally “teaching woman”) may also be used to refer to a female teacher specifically.

